oston University School of Public Health, Department of Environmental Health</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B12"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Susser</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The logic in ecological: I. The logic of analysis</article-title><source>Am J Public Health</source><year>1994</year><volume>84</volume><fpage>825</fpage><lpage>829</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8179056</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B13"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Goodman</surname><given-names>LA</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Ecological regressions and the behavior of individuals</article-title><source>Am Sociol Rev</source><year>1953</year><volume>18</volume><fpage>663</fpage><lpage>664</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.2307/2088121</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B14"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wakefield</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Ecological inference for 2 &#x000d7; 2 tables</article-title><source>J R Statist Soc A</source><year>2004</year><volume>167</volume><fpage>385</fpage><lpage>445</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1111/j.1467-985x.2004.02046_1.x</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B15"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Greenland</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Morgenstern</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Ecological bias, confounding, and effect modification</article-title><source>Int J Epidemiol</source><year>1989</year><volume>18</volume><fpage>269</fpage><lpage>274</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2656561</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1093/ije/18.1.269</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B16"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Palmquist</surname><given-names>BL</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Ecological Inference, Aggregate Data Analysis of United States Elections, and the Socialist Party of America</article-title><source>PhD thesis</source><year>1993</year><publisher-name>University of California Berkeley, Political Science Department</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B17"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Robinson</surname><given-names>WS</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Ecological correlation and the behavior of individuals</article-title><source>Am Sociol Rev</source><year>1950</year><volume>15</volume><fpage>351</fpage><lpage>357</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.2307/2087176</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B18"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Duncan</surname><given-names>OD</given-names></name><name><surname>Cuzzort</surname><given-names>RP</given-names></name><name><surname>Duncan</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name></person-group><source>Statistical Geography: Problems in Analyzing Areal Data</source><year>1961</year><publisher-name>Glencoe, IL: Free Press</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B19"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Piantadosi</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Byar</surname><given-names>DP</given-names></name><name><surname>Green</surname><given-names>SB</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The ecological fallacy</article-title><source>Am J Epidemiol</source><year>1988</year><volume>127</volume><fpage>893</fpage><lpage>904</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">3282433</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B20"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Brenner</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Savitz</surname><given-names>DA</given-names></name><name><surname>J&#x000f6;ckel</surname><given-names>K-H</given-names></name><name><surname>Greenland</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Effects of nondifferential exposure misclassification in ecologic studies</article-title><source>Am J Epidemiol</source><year>1992</year><volume>135</volume><fpage>85</fpage><lpage>95</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1736664</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B21"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wakefield</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Sensitivity analysis for ecological regression</article-title><source>Biometrics</source><year>2003</year><volume>59</volume><fpage>9</fpage><lpage>17</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12762436</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1111/1541-0420.00002</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B22"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Webster</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Commentary: Does the spectre of ecologic bias haunt epidemiology?</article-title><source>Int J Epidemiol</source><year>2002</year><volume>31</volume><fpage>161</fpage><lpage>162</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11914313</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1093/ije/31.1.161</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B23"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Lagarde</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Pershagen</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Parallel analysis of individual and ecologic data on residential radon, cofactors, and lung cancer in Sweden</article-title><source>Am J Epidemiol</source><year>1999</year><volume>149</volume><fpage>268</fpage><lpage>274</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9927223</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B24"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bj&#x000f6;rk</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Str&#x000f6;mberg</surname><given-names>U</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Effects of systematic exposure assessment errors in partially ecologic case-control studies</article-title><source>Int J Epidemiol</source><year>2002</year><volume>31</volume><fpage>154</fpage><lpage>160</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11914312</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1093/ije/31.1.154</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B25"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Plummer</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Clayton</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Estimation of population exposure in ecological studies</article-title><source>J R Statist Soc B</source><year>1996</year><volume>58</volume><fpage>113</fpage><lpage>126</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B26"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Best</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Cockings</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Bennett</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Wakefield</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Elliott</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Ecological Regression Analysis of Environmental Benzene Exposure and Childhood Leukaemia: Sensitivity to Data Inaccuracies, Geographical Scale and Ecological Bias</article-title><source>J R Statist Soc A</source><year>2001</year><volume>164</volume><fpage>155</fpage><lpage>174</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1111/1467-985X.00194</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B27"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Richardson</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Montfo